icine , Stanford, California."}]}}, {"fullName": "Kosaric N", "lastName": "Kosaric", "firstName": "Nina", "initials": "N", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Surgery, Stanford University School of Medicine , Stanford, California."}]}}, {"fullName": "Bonham CA", "lastName": "Bonham", "firstName": "Clark A", "initials": "CA", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Surgery, Stanford University School of Medicine , Stanford, California."}]}}, {"fullName": "Gurtner GC", "lastName": "Gurtner", "firstName": "Geoffrey C", "initials": "GC", "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Surgery, Stanford University School of Medicine , Stanford, California."}]}}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "recommended_reviews", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"issue": "1", "volume": "99", "journalIssueId": 2731077, "dateOfPublication": "2019 Jan", "monthOfPublication": 1, "yearOfPublication": 2019, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Physiol Rev", "title": "Physiological reviews", "essn": "1522-1210", "issn": "0031-9333", "nlmid": "0231714", "isoabbreviation": "Physiol Rev"}, "printPublicationDate": "2019-01-01"}, "pubYear": "2019", "pageInfo": "665-706", "abstractText": "Wound healing is one of the most complex processes in the human body. It involves the spatial and temporal synchronization of a variety of cell types with distinct roles in the phases of hemostasis, inflammation, growth, re-epithelialization, and remodeling. With the evolution of single cell technologies, it has been possible to uncover phenotypic and functional heterogeneity within several of these cell types. There have also been discoveries of rare, stem cell subsets within the skin, which are unipotent in the uninjured state, but become multipotent following skin injury. Unraveling the roles of each of these cell types and their interactions with each other is important in understanding the mechanisms of normal wound closure. Changes in the microenvironment including alterations in mechanical forces, oxygen levels, chemokines, extracellular matrix and growth factor synthesis directly impact cellular recruitment and activation, leading to impaired states of wound healing. Single cell technologies can be used to decipher these cellular alterations in diseased states such as in chronic wounds and hypertrophic scarring so that effective therapeutic solutions for healing wounds can be developed.", "affiliation": "Department of Surgery, Stanford University School of Medicine , Stanford, California.", "publicationStatus": "ppublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Print", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["review-article", "Review", "Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "HHS | NIH | National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK)", "grantId": "R01-DK074095-13", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIA NIH HHS", "acronym": "AG", "grantId": "R01 AG025016", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIDDK NIH HHS", "acronym": "DK", "grantId": "R01 DK074095", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Blood Platelets", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "metabolism"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Extracellular Matrix", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "metabolism"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Skin", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "ME", "qualifierName": "metabolism"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "abbreviation": "PA", "qualifierName": "pathology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Animals"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN"